const CONTENT = {"sessions": [{"n": 1, "title": "Start with a Problem and Explore the Innovation Center", "goal": "Explain the one-project journey, identify real incidents and demonstrate safe team routines.", "exit": "Team agreement, two or three incident-based problems, verified tool constraints and each learner’s first record. Each learner supplies one own incident, one named output and an accurate safety/stop explanation.", "individual": "One own incident, one named task and an accurate safety/stop explanation.", "example": "", "questions": [{"q": "Why describe an incident before choosing a product?", "a": "An incident identifies a user, task and difficulty that the team can investigate."}, {"q": "Does seeing a machine on the tour allow you to use it?", "a": "No. The actual job still requires the centre's checks, an authorised operator and supervision."}, {"q": "What should a help request show?", "a": "The exact difficulty, what the team has safely tried, and the question it needs answered."}], "notes": "Students can: Explain the one-project journey, identify real incidents and demonstrate safe team routines.\nCore exit evidence: Team agreement, two or three incident-based problems, verified tool constraints and each learner’s first record. Each learner supplies one own incident, one named output and an accurate safety/stop explanation.\nEach learner: One own incident, one named task and an accurate safety/stop explanation.\nPrint pages 1 and 3 for each learner and page 2 once per team. Personal pages can be printed back to back. Keep the shared page with the ongoing team record; do not require another report.\nBring pens and the three worksheet pages. Confirm the school’s actual orientation route, safe travel time, tool rules, authorised staff and storage. Check recording permissions. The school controls facility access, machine queues and production time; the curriculum does not allocate them. No machines are needed for the written tasks.\nKeep the class together on the approved route. No independent machine trials. Approved project scope is not permission to operate equipment. Stop the affected activity for a safety concern. Use roles or user codes; do not record people without school permission. Follow the school’s stop, storage and shutdown procedures.\nSession 1 Teaching notes and assessment\nIf access changes: use only school-approved information or an authorised in-room explanation of an actual tool. Record the source. This does not count as a completed visit or facility use. Keep missing evidence pending and continue the problem record or safe routines.\nTeach the ruler example aloud: separate what was seen from a possible reason. Show one step and its answer space, then ask a learner to restate it. Introduce future codes only when needed.\nLanguage support: use oral rehearsal, short notes, labelled sketches or an agreed spoken response. Keep the same skill and identify each learner’s own evidence.\nTiming: protect safety, each learner’s evidence and the final ten-minute close. Use 70–80 for clarification or movement delays; cut repeated explanation or the optional third problem first.\nQuick check with suggested answers\n1. Why describe an incident before choosing a product? An incident identifies a user, task and difficulty that the team can investigate.\n2. Does seeing a machine on the tour allow you to use it? No. The actual job still requires the centre's checks, an authorised operator and supervision.\n3. What should a help request show? The exact difficulty, what the team has safely tried, and the question it needs answered.\nCheck: each learner gives their own incident before team discussion. Redirect product-first ideas with “What happened?” Verify safety answers against the school’s rules. Collect evidence during table rounds, not another full round of presentations.\nS01 assessment out of 10\nT1 + T2: team evidence /4. I1 + I2: individual work and reasoning /4. C1: individual checking and response /2. For each row, 2 = both conditions, 1 = one, 0 = neither in assessable work; NE = evidence pending.", "route": [{"time": "0–7", "work": "Show the project journey and ruler example. Ask: “Which sentence is what we saw? Which is a possible reason?” Explain the five assessment checks in plain language."}, {"time": "7–19", "work": "Individual page 1. Each learner recalls a genuine school incident. Offer oral rehearsal or a labelled drawing before writing."}, {"time": "19–35", "work": "Teams share, group two or three problems and complete page 2 steps 2–4. Agree the attention signal, records and waiting task. Give the visit instructions."}, {"time": "35–60", "work": "School-confirmed orientation, including movement. Keep the class together; note actual tool rules on page 2 step 5. No independent trials or machine operation."}, {"time": "60–70", "work": "Return to tables. Each learner completes page 3 step 6. Check rules with the authorised source rather than accepting guesses."}, {"time": "70–80", "work": "Flex time for questions, transition delays and unfinished evidence. If ready, improve one investigation question. Do not add a second task just to fill time."}, {"time": "80–90", "work": "Each learner completes step 7. Collect each learner’s personal record, retain the team record and rehearse safe close. Note missing evidence and the next step."}], "rubric": [{"id": "T1 Team", "a": "The candidate problems describe actual users doing a task.", "b": "The list separates a difficulty from a preferred product."}, {"id": "T2 Team", "a": "The team record assigns specific first tasks to members.", "b": "The recorded tool/process options and restrictions match the tour."}, {"id": "I1 Individual", "a": "The learner provides their own incident with enough context to understand it.", "b": "The learner distinguishes what was observed from an interpretation."}, {"id": "I2 Individual", "a": "The learner accurately identifies required tool authorization or supervision.", "b": "The learner explains a relevant stop condition and safe response."}, {"id": "C1 Individual", "a": "The learner checks their first record against the stated task.", "b": "The learner records a specific correction or a justified confirmation."}]}, {"n": 2, "title": "Investigate Users and Record the Starting Situation", "goal": "Gather traceable evidence using neutral questions and a relevant observation or measurement.", "exit": "Use S02. Traceable research records, a starting measure or clearly stated comparison gap, and one candidate need.", "individual": "One genuine observation, user response or measurement and a neutral question they contributed.", "example": "“Would a new tray help?” leads the user. “Show me how you find a ruler” invites an action. Record “opened three drawers” as what happened; “labels may be unclear” is a possible explanation, not a finding.", "questions": [{"q": "Which is evidence: 'the tray is confusing' or 'the user opened three compartments before finding the item'?", "a": "The second is an observation; the first is an interpretation to investigate."}, {"q": "What makes a starting measure useful?", "a": "It relates to the actual user task and records the method and conditions so a later comparison is possible."}, {"q": "What if no target user is available?", "a": "Record that gap, complete the approved evidence task and arrange another access opportunity; do not invent an interview."}], "notes": "Students can: Gather traceable evidence using neutral questions and a relevant observation or measurement.\nCore exit evidence: Use S02. Traceable research records, a starting measure or clearly stated comparison gap, and one candidate need.\nEach learner: One genuine observation, user response or measurement and a neutral question they contributed.\nPrepare: S02; the team's S01 incident list; an approved whole-class observation zone or in-room users; neutral question examples and permitted measuring tools. Confirm access and an alternative evidence activity before the lesson.\nTeacher control: Use one visible area; do not send teams independently around school. During minutes 50–70, review up to six tables for about three minutes each.\nSession 2 Teaching notes and assessment\nSafe parallel work or optional stretch: Repeat a doubtful measurement, check source labels or improve the next question. Cancelled access is recorded; internal observations are not labelled target-user interviews.\nIf user access changes: practise neutral questions and inspect genuine available records. Label this as preparation or internal evidence; target-user evidence stays pending.\nTeaching example: “Would a new tray help?” leads the user. “Show me how you find a ruler” invites an action. Record “opened three drawers” as what happened; “labels may be unclear” is a possible explanation, not a finding.\nFlex within 5–15: use up to five minutes for modelling, a learner restatement and clarification. Avoid repeating the full project introduction. Preserve the agreed user-access window and individual evidence.\nQuick check with suggested answers\n1. Which is evidence: 'the tray is confusing' or 'the user opened three compartments before finding the item'? The second is an observation; the first is an interpretation to investigate.\n2. What makes a starting measure useful? It relates to the actual user task and records the method and conditions so a later comparison is possible.\n3. What if no target user is available? Record that gap, complete the approved evidence task and arrange another access opportunity; do not invent an interview.\nWatch for: Challenge leading questions and unsupported summaries. Ask students to point to the source record before accepting a claim about users.\nS02 assessment out of 10\nT1 + T2: team evidence /4. I1 + I2: individual work and reasoning /4. C1: individual checking and response /2. For each row, 2 = both conditions, 1 = one, 0 = neither in assessable work; NE = evidence pending.", "route": [{"time": "0–5", "work": "Select one candidate problem and read the evidence target."}, {"time": "5–15", "work": "Practise a neutral question and a short observation using a guided example."}, {"time": "15–25", "work": "Plan the user task, questions, context and named collectors."}, {"time": "25–50", "work": "Collect genuine school-user evidence in one approved supervised zone or in-room arrangement."}, {"time": "50–70", "work": "Separate observations, quotations and interpretations while the teacher visits tables."}, {"time": "70–80", "work": "Record a starting measure or functional benchmark and missing perspectives."}, {"time": "80–90", "work": "Submit evidence with source/context and an individual explanation; reset the room."}], "rubric": [{"id": "T1 Team", "a": "Records include enough source and context to trace the information.", "b": "Observed actions or words are separated from team interpretations."}, {"id": "T2 Team", "a": "The starting measure or benchmark is relevant to the user task.", "b": "The record identifies missing perspectives or limitations without inventing evidence."}, {"id": "I1 Individual", "a": "The learner's research contribution is identifiable in a record.", "b": "The learner explains accurately how that information was collected."}, {"id": "I2 Individual", "a": "The learner uses a question that does not steer the user toward a product.", "b": "The learner explains what the response can and cannot support."}, {"id": "C1 Individual", "a": "The learner checks a source, question or measurement for a specific weakness.", "b": "The learner records the check result and a correction or justified next question."}]}, {"n": 3, "title": "Define the Problem and Review the Evidence", "goal": "Set one focused problem and two testable criteria; respond to another team's evidence challenge.", "exit": "Use S03. One focused brief, two test criteria, peer review with response and M1 Ready/Revise/Hold status.", "individual": "Explain an important evidence item and one cause hypothesis or limitation.", "example": "Illustrative criterion: “A user finds the correct ruler within 15 seconds, without help.” Test: same drawer setup, task and timer; record time, wrong choices and help. The team must choose its own justified target from its actual evidence.", "questions": [{"q": "What must a focused problem statement identify?", "a": "A reachable user group, the task or situation, the difficulty and supporting evidence."}, {"q": "How can a criterion be checked?", "a": "State the observable result, task and conditions, then explain how to record the outcome."}, {"q": "Must the team accept every peer suggestion?", "a": "No. It must examine the evidence and record a reasoned acceptance, rejection or revision."}], "notes": "Students can: Set one focused problem and two testable criteria; respond to another team's evidence challenge.\nCore exit evidence: Use S03. One focused brief, two test criteria, peer review with response and M1 Ready/Revise/Hold status.\nEach learner: Explain an important evidence item and one cause hypothesis or limitation.\nPrepare: S03; research records and any starting measures; the M1 gate; paired-team review order; a timer and the status board. Prepare one example showing the difference between a need and a product.\nTeacher control: Check scope and test feasibility during the work; use the last work block for a concise gate check, not a second long presentation.\nSession 3 Teaching notes and assessment\nSafe parallel work or optional stretch: Rehearse the measurement, locate conflicting evidence or improve the problem statement. No production starts.\nIf evidence is too weak: narrow the question, mark the claim as unconfirmed and name one next observation. Do not approve a product merely because the team likes it.\nPeer-review arrangement: Paired teams; one brief presentation then exchange records. For an odd number, use one three-team review cycle.\nReviewer questions\n1. Which evidence ID supports the named need? Point to the recorded observation.\n2. Which part is still an assumption, and what could check it?\n3. Could you carry out each criterion's test from the written method? Identify one missing unit, condition or step.\nReceiving team: For one comment, state accept / reject / revise, cite the reason and show the changed wording or evidence request.\nTeaching example: Illustrative criterion: “A user finds the correct ruler within 15 seconds, without help.” Test: same drawer setup, task and timer; record time, wrong choices and help. The team must choose its own justified target from its actual evidence.\nFlex within 5–20: allow up to five minutes for a worked criterion and clarification, replacing repeated whole-team reporting. Each learner still explains their own finding. Keep the two criteria and peer response.\nQuick check with suggested answers\n1. What must a focused problem statement identify? A reachable user group, the task or situation, the difficulty and supporting evidence.\n2. How can a criterion be checked? State the observable result, task and conditions, then explain how to record the outcome.\n3. Must the team accept every peer suggestion? No. It must examine the evidence and record a reasoned acceptance, rejection or revision.\nWatch for: Keep one user group and one pilot context. A guessed cause remains a hypothesis until evidence supports it; broad wording should return for revision.\nS03 assessment out of 10\nT1 + T2: team evidence /4. I1 + I2: individual work and reasoning /4. C1: individual checking and response /2. For each row, 2 = both conditions, 1 = one, 0 = neither in assessable work; NE = evidence pending.", "route": [{"time": "0–5", "work": "Read the M1 gate and reopen the source evidence."}, {"time": "5–20", "work": "Each member explains one finding and separates a symptom from a possible cause."}, {"time": "20–35", "work": "Choose one user group and pilot context; write an evidence-linked problem statement."}, {"time": "35–50", "work": "Set two observable criteria and specify how each will be tested."}, {"time": "50–65", "work": "Present the brief to a paired team; reviewers check evidence links, scope and criteria."}, {"time": "65–80", "work": "Respond to feedback and revise; the teacher checks each team's gate status in table visits."}, {"time": "80–90", "work": "Save the scoped brief, review response and individual evidence; complete the Resume line."}], "rubric": [{"id": "T1 Team", "a": "The problem statement is supported by identifiable research evidence.", "b": "The scope is one reachable user group and one pilot context."}, {"id": "T2 Team", "a": "Both criteria describe observable performance relevant to the need.", "b": "Each criterion has a feasible test and a way to record its result."}, {"id": "I1 Individual", "a": "The learner explains what one actual evidence item shows.", "b": "The learner identifies a justified limit or unanswered question."}, {"id": "I2 Individual", "a": "The learner distinguishes an observed symptom from a possible cause.", "b": "The learner states evidence that could check the proposed cause."}, {"id": "C1 Individual", "a": "The learner gives or examines a specific evidence-linked review comment.", "b": "The learner records a reasoned response and resulting action or justified non-change."}]}, {"n": 4, "title": "Develop Two Approaches and Quick Models", "goal": "Generate distinct solutions and use low-cost models to investigate an uncertainty.", "exit": "Use S04. Two distinct team approaches, Prototype V0 models and preliminary trial findings with simulation limits.", "individual": "Two own sketches and an explanation of their operation and differences.", "example": "Model the difference: a larger handle and a sliding tab may work differently; two handle colours do not. Ask students to label “What the user does”, “How it works” and “What we need to find out” on each sketch.", "questions": [{"q": "What makes two approaches meaningfully different?", "a": "They solve the need or work in different ways; a colour change alone is insufficient."}, {"q": "What question will the quick model answer?", "a": "Name a specific uncertainty, such as fit, grip or a sequence of use, and an observable trial."}, {"q": "Can a paper model establish final material strength?", "a": "No. Record what it demonstrates and what remains simulated or untested."}], "notes": "Students can: Generate distinct solutions and use low-cost models to investigate an uncertainty.\nCore exit evidence: Use S04. Two distinct team approaches, Prototype V0 models and preliminary trial findings with simulation limits.\nEach learner: Two own sketches and an explanation of their operation and differences.\nPrepare: S04; the S03 brief and criteria; approved low-cost modelling materials, drawing tools and reference sources. State the limits of each modelling material before building begins.\nTeacher control: During modelling, check that each model answers a question and claims do not exceed its material or simulation limits.\nSession 4 Teaching notes and assessment\nSafe parallel work or optional stretch: Measure, prepare the common test task or annotate sources while someone else builds; early teams test one uncertain condition.\nIf materials are missing: make an approved paper or card model of the uncertain part. State what it can show and what it cannot prove; do not claim final strength from paper.\nTeaching example: Model the difference: a larger handle and a sliding tab may work differently; two handle colours do not. Ask students to label “What the user does”, “How it works” and “What we need to find out” on each sketch.\nFlex within 20–30: use up to five minutes for clarification and materials movement. Reduce discussion of decorative choices. Keep each learner’s two sketches and two distinct team approaches.\nQuick check with suggested answers\n1. What makes two approaches meaningfully different? They solve the need or work in different ways; a colour change alone is insufficient.\n2. What question will the quick model answer? Name a specific uncertainty, such as fit, grip or a sequence of use, and an observable trial.\n3. Can a paper model establish final material strength? No. Record what it demonstrates and what remains simulated or untested.\nWatch for: Keep the first models within the same project. Require two own sketches from every learner and evidence of what each model was used to check.\nS04 assessment out of 10\nT1 + T2: team evidence /4. I1 + I2: individual work and reasoning /4. C1: individual checking and response /2. For each row, 2 = both conditions, 1 = one, 0 = neither in assessable work; NE = evidence pending.", "route": [{"time": "0–5", "work": "Select one uncertainty from the approved brief."}, {"time": "5–20", "work": "Each learner sketches two meaningfully different approaches."}, {"time": "20–30", "work": "Select two team approaches and identify original/adapted elements."}, {"time": "30–50", "work": "Build simple Prototype V0 models of the uncertain part of each approach."}, {"time": "50–65", "work": "Try the same short task with both models and record what each can show."}, {"time": "65–80", "work": "Compare preliminary findings against the criteria; identify remaining unknowns."}, {"time": "80–90", "work": "Save sketches/models and individual records; store materials."}], "rubric": [{"id": "T1 Team", "a": "The two approaches solve the need in meaningfully different ways.", "b": "Each approach explains the user's action and the proposed response."}, {"id": "T2 Team", "a": "The quick models investigate a stated uncertainty.", "b": "The record distinguishes demonstrated behaviour from simulated or untested features."}, {"id": "I1 Individual", "a": "The learner shows two own sketches rather than only the team's final drawing.", "b": "The learner explains an operational difference between the sketches."}, {"id": "I2 Individual", "a": "The learner identifies an idea they developed or adapted.", "b": "The learner credits a relevant source or accurately states the work is their own."}, {"id": "C1 Individual", "a": "The learner checks one model feature with an observable trial.", "b": "The learner records the result and an implication for the next design decision."}]}, {"n": 5, "title": "Compare Models through Peer Trials", "goal": "Use a peer task trial to select an approach and justify what should change.", "exit": "Use S05. Evidence-based approach selection, comparative trial records and a visible response to peer feedback.", "individual": "One owned trial record and a reasoned recommendation linked to a criterion.", "example": "Illustrative comparison: A takes 12 seconds with no help; B takes 9 seconds but needs one prompt. “B is faster” is incomplete. Compare the same task, help and criterion before choosing. These numbers are teaching examples, not student results.", "questions": [{"q": "Why use the same task for both models?", "a": "It makes the comparison more meaningful; record remaining differences in conditions or help."}, {"q": "Are classmates automatically the target users?", "a": "No. Label them as peer reviewers unless they genuinely match the selected user group."}, {"q": "What should support the chosen approach?", "a": "A specific trial result linked to the user criteria and actual resource limits."}], "notes": "Students can: Use a peer task trial to select an approach and justify what should change.\nCore exit evidence: Use S05. Evidence-based approach selection, comparative trial records and a visible response to peer feedback.\nEach learner: One owned trial record and a reasoned recommendation linked to a criterion.\nPrepare: S05; both quick models; one common task and criteria; a visible paired-team trial layout; the peer-review order and permitted measuring tools. Clear all models for low-risk use.\nTeacher control: All trials stay in the visible area and use low-risk models. Peers are model reviewers; label them accurately rather than assuming they are target users.\nSession 5 Teaching notes and assessment\nSafe parallel work or optional stretch: Prepare the second model's task, check a doubtful result or annotate a limitation; do not decorate or add another function.\nIf a model breaks: record it, check whether the other model can be tried safely and identify what evidence remains missing. Do not invent an A/B result. Continue the shared comparison record from S04.\nPeer-review arrangement: Paired-team model trial; use a three-team cycle for an odd number.\nReviewer questions\n1. At which exact step did you pause, fail or need help? Record the step and help.\n2. For criterion 1 or 2, what did you observe with A and with B under the same task?\n3. Which result supports a suggested change, and what has the model not yet proved?\nReceiving team: Choose one finding, accept/reject/revise with evidence, and identify the exact design change or next check.\nTeaching example: Illustrative comparison: A takes 12 seconds with no help; B takes 9 seconds but needs one prompt. “B is faster” is incomplete. Compare the same task, help and criterion before choosing. These numbers are teaching examples, not student results.\nFlex within 0–10: reserve up to five minutes for moving models and clarifying the common task. Avoid a second long introduction. Protect the actual trials and each learner’s explanation.\nQuick check with suggested answers\n1. Why use the same task for both models? It makes the comparison more meaningful; record remaining differences in conditions or help.\n2. Are classmates automatically the target users? No. Label them as peer reviewers unless they genuinely match the selected user group.\n3. What should support the chosen approach? A specific trial result linked to the user criteria and actual resource limits.\nWatch for: Replace preference-only comments with the exact step, observed result and help given. A review is complete when the receiving team records its response.\nS05 assessment out of 10\nT1 + T2: team evidence /4. I1 + I2: individual work and reasoning /4. C1: individual checking and response /2. For each row, 2 = both conditions, 1 = one, 0 = neither in assessable work; NE = evidence pending.", "route": [{"time": "0–10", "work": "Prepare two models, the same trial task and criteria."}, {"time": "10–20", "work": "Rehearse neutral instructions and assign test/recording roles."}, {"time": "20–45", "work": "Paired teams try the models using the written task; record pauses, errors, help and relevant measures."}, {"time": "45–60", "work": "Compare both approaches against the same criteria and resource limits."}, {"time": "60–75", "work": "Select an approach, respond to peer findings and revise its key sketch or model."}, {"time": "75–80", "work": "Each learner explains one decision changed or confirmed by evidence."}, {"time": "80–90", "work": "Save the comparison and response; label Prototype V0 evidence and reset the room."}], "rubric": [{"id": "T1 Team", "a": "The two approaches are compared through the same relevant task.", "b": "The comparison records actual outcomes and assistance, not only preferences."}, {"id": "T2 Team", "a": "The chosen approach is justified against user criteria and resources.", "b": "The decision identifies a remaining uncertainty or limitation."}, {"id": "I1 Individual", "a": "The learner's own trial observation or measurement is traceable.", "b": "The learner accurately explains what happened without overstating the model."}, {"id": "I2 Individual", "a": "The learner recommends an approach or change using a specific result.", "b": "The learner explains how it relates to a success criterion."}, {"id": "C1 Individual", "a": "The learner identifies a precise peer finding rather than generic praise.", "b": "The learner documents a reasoned response and a resulting revision or next test."}]}, {"n": 6, "title": "Approve the Scope and Production Plan", "goal": "Make a feasible plan for one function, verified facility use, individual tasks and user testing.", "exit": "Use S06. A scoped plan with actual resource/time constraints, confirmed Innovation Center route, test method and functional fallback.", "individual": "Show own prior work and state a specific next output and its purpose.", "example": "Explain the plan through six questions: What must it do? What will we make? What do we need? What must be ready first? Who will produce which part? How will we test it? “Safe work while waiting” means a useful task that needs no unavailable machine.", "questions": [{"q": "What is the core function?", "a": "The necessary action the team must test to address the user's need; optional features can be removed."}, {"q": "Does scope approval mean a machine can start?", "a": "No. A separate job-specific safety and readiness check is required."}, {"q": "What makes a next task clear?", "a": "A named person, a concrete output, its purpose and any dependency or permission needed."}], "notes": "Students can: Make a feasible plan for one function, verified facility use, individual tasks and user testing.\nCore exit evidence: Use S06. A scoped plan with actual resource/time constraints, confirmed Innovation Center route, test method and functional fallback.\nEach learner: Show own prior work and state a specific next output and its purpose.\nPrepare: S06; the S05 comparison; school-confirmed equipment access, approved material allowance and file requirements; a short scope-review order. Verify the user-test route and fallback.\nTeacher control: Up to six three-minute scope reviews plus transitions. Actual machine/job safety checks are separate and never forced into this time limit.\nSession 6 Teaching notes and assessment\nSafe parallel work or optional stretch: Run a paper fit check, check material/file completeness or practise the test. Unconfirmed resource access remains Hold for that operation.\nIf access is unconfirmed: mark only the affected operation Hold. Check the design on paper, prepare files or rehearse the test. Do not allocate a queue, machine duration or new slot.\nTeaching example: Explain the plan through six questions: What must it do? What will we make? What do we need? What must be ready first? Who will produce which part? How will we test it? “Safe work while waiting” means a useful task that needs no unavailable machine.\nFlex within 10–25: use up to five minutes for explaining the plan headings. Students fill the existing record from prior work, not a new long proposal. During 55–70, remove extras before cutting required evidence.\nQuick check with suggested answers\n1. What is the core function? The necessary action the team must test to address the user's need; optional features can be removed.\n2. Does scope approval mean a machine can start? No. A separate job-specific safety and readiness check is required.\n3. What makes a next task clear? A named person, a concrete output, its purpose and any dependency or permission needed.\nWatch for: Do not approve a build that depends on unconfirmed school access. Keep the affected operation on Hold and assign a useful safe task.\nS06 assessment out of 10\nT1 + T2: team evidence /4. I1 + I2: individual work and reasoning /4. C1: individual checking and response /2. For each row, 2 = both conditions, 1 = one, 0 = neither in assessable work; NE = evidence pending.", "route": [{"time": "0–10", "work": "Separate the core function from extras and review the Session 9 testable-build deadline."}, {"time": "10–25", "work": "Complete the proposal in the existing report: design, evidence, resources, risks, tasks, test and fallback."}, {"time": "25–35", "work": "Swap plans for a completeness and test-instruction check."}, {"time": "35–55", "work": "Use scheduled scope reviews; waiting teams check dimensions, files/materials and the test dry run."}, {"time": "55–70", "work": "Revise the proposal and reduce unconfirmed dependencies."}, {"time": "70–80", "work": "Recheck conditions and record school-confirmed access or pending dependencies and each learner’s next output."}, {"time": "80–90", "work": "Save M2 status, individual next outputs and the Resume line."}], "rubric": [{"id": "T1 Team", "a": "The proposal links the chosen core function to user evidence.", "b": "The plan removes unnecessary scope and identifies the team's own development."}, {"id": "T2 Team", "a": "Resources, supervised production route and time limits are grounded in confirmed arrangements or explicitly unresolved conditions.", "b": "The test plan and functional fallback are feasible within the remaining course."}, {"id": "I1 Individual", "a": "The learner shows their own prior work and explains a design decision.", "b": "The decision is linked to evidence or a real constraint."}, {"id": "I2 Individual", "a": "The learner names a concrete next output they own.", "b": "The learner explains its purpose and any dependency before starting."}, {"id": "C1 Individual", "a": "The learner checks a dimension, resource dependency or test instruction.", "b": "The learner records the result and resolves or explicitly escalates the issue."}]}, {"n": 7, "title": "Check Readiness and Make a Critical Part", "goal": "Verify design/material readiness and learn from a small production or functional trial.", "exit": "Use S07. Readiness evidence, a critical-part trial, measured result and actual tool/process-use record.", "individual": "One check, part or trial result with an explanation of purpose and safe method.", "example": "Illustrative fit check: the drawing needs a 10 mm opening; the actual opening measures 9 mm. Record intended size, measured size and unit together, then explain the mismatch. Do not substitute these example measurements for real checks.", "questions": [{"q": "Why try a small part first?", "a": "It can reveal a critical fit or function problem before using materials on the complete build."}, {"q": "What should happen when a trial differs from the design?", "a": "Record the observation, check a possible cause and identify the next safe correction or check."}, {"q": "What do waiting teams do?", "a": "Their assigned assembly, fit-check, test-preparation or evidence task at the approved work area."}], "notes": "Students can: Verify design/material readiness and learn from a small production or functional trial.\nCore exit evidence: Use S07. Readiness evidence, a critical-part trial, measured result and actual tool/process-use record.\nEach learner: One check, part or trial result with an explanation of purpose and safe method.\nPrepare: S07; the approved S06 plan; designs/files, approved materials and readiness checks; the school’s current access instructions; a safe parallel-work area and storage. Do not assume when a machine job will finish.\nTeacher control: Use the safe time required for each check. While supervising production, close routine help requests and do not walk away to other teams.\nSession 7 Teaching notes and assessment\nSafe parallel work or optional stretch: Baseline assembly, fit checks, test setup or evidence captions. A delayed job triggers the functional fallback rather than team inactivity.\nIf fabrication is delayed: carry out approved fit checks, prepare a test or label genuine evidence. Keep required facility use pending. Continue the same build record in S08.\nTeaching example: Illustrative fit check: the drawing needs a 10 mm opening; the actual opening measures 9 mm. Record intended size, measured size and unit together, then explain the mismatch. Do not substitute these example measurements for real checks.\nFlex within 0–10: clarify the required part and each learner’s output. Adjust non-machine explanation or optional detail if delayed. The lesson plan does not set or move school machine times.\nQuick check with suggested answers\n1. Why try a small part first? It can reveal a critical fit or function problem before using materials on the complete build.\n2. What should happen when a trial differs from the design? Record the observation, check a possible cause and identify the next safe correction or check.\n3. What do waiting teams do? Their assigned assembly, fit-check, test-preparation or evidence task at the approved work area.\nWatch for: Keep routine consultations closed during active machine supervision. Stop any arrangement that prevents safe supervision of the rest of the class.\nS07 assessment out of 10\nT1 + T2: team evidence /4. I1 + I2: individual work and reasoning /4. C1: individual checking and response /2. For each row, 2 = both conditions, 1 = one, 0 = neither in assessable work; NE = evidence pending.", "route": [{"time": "0–10", "work": "Check approved scope, today's component and individual outputs."}, {"time": "10–25", "work": "Check a critical dimension/joint with a low-cost trial."}, {"time": "25–40", "work": "Prepare for the school’s required job-specific checks. Use its access instructions; no operation starts without permission."}, {"time": "40–65", "work": "Continue school-approved making when available; otherwise complete fit checks, assembly preparation or test records. Machine timing remains the school’s decision."}, {"time": "65–80", "work": "Measure or test completed parts and choose one correction if needed."}, {"time": "80–90", "work": "Follow required shutdown timing, store labelled work and record version/results."}], "rubric": [{"id": "T1 Team", "a": "Relevant dimensions, materials and interfaces are checked before the affected build.", "b": "Issues are resolved or kept explicitly on hold before production."}, {"id": "T2 Team", "a": "A small trial produces observable or measured evidence.", "b": "The result is used to decide the next build action."}, {"id": "I1 Individual", "a": "The learner shows a specific check, part or trial they contributed.", "b": "The learner explains its relationship to the core function."}, {"id": "I2 Individual", "a": "The learner explains a relevant safe working or stop requirement.", "b": "The learner accurately identifies the actual authorization/supervision needed."}, {"id": "C1 Individual", "a": "The learner compares an actual trial outcome against its intended result.", "b": "The learner records a correction or a justified decision to proceed."}]}, {"n": 8, "title": "Build and Assemble the First Functional Version", "goal": "Assemble Prototype V1 through named work packages and check critical interfaces.", "exit": "Use S08. Prototype V1 or a safe testable critical section, assembly evidence, interface check and clear unfinished dependencies.", "individual": "One owned build/assembly/check output and an evidence-based explanation of a mismatch or design choice.", "example": "Explain “interface” as where parts join or work together. Check one connection, movement or sequence. Mark it working, failing or not yet tested, and point to the result that supports that label.", "questions": [{"q": "What does an interface check examine?", "a": "A relevant connection or interaction, such as fit, movement, alignment or the order of use."}, {"q": "Does completed assembly prove that the function works?", "a": "No. Test the core action against the stated criteria and distinguish untested sections."}, {"q": "What should a build record explain?", "a": "The version, each important change and its reason, current function and unresolved dependencies."}], "notes": "Students can: Assemble Prototype V1 through named work packages and check critical interfaces.\nCore exit evidence: Use S08. Prototype V1 or a safe testable critical section, assembly evidence, interface check and clear unfinished dependencies.\nEach learner: One owned build/assembly/check output and an evidence-based explanation of a mismatch or design choice.\nPrepare: S08; checked components or a safe functional fallback; the current design and approved assembly tools; interface checks, version labels and storage. Identify outstanding dependencies before class.\nTeacher control: Keep production supervision separate from table consultations. Inspect unsafe sections before any use; do not treat an attractive assembly as functional proof.\nSession 8 Teaching notes and assessment\nSafe parallel work or optional stretch: Measure fit, assemble the baseline route, prepare instructions or check component labels. Do not invent finished production evidence.\nIf a component is unavailable: assemble or test a safe independent section, prepare instructions or check dimensions. Keep the dependency visible. Continue S07’s record rather than copying it.\nTeaching example: Explain “interface” as where parts join or work together. Check one connection, movement or sequence. Mark it working, failing or not yet tested, and point to the result that supports that label.\nFlex within 5–15: allow up to five minutes for materials movement and clarifying outputs. Keep the core-action check and personal build evidence. School production arrangements remain unchanged.\nQuick check with suggested answers\n1. What does an interface check examine? A relevant connection or interaction, such as fit, movement, alignment or the order of use.\n2. Does completed assembly prove that the function works? No. Test the core action against the stated criteria and distinguish untested sections.\n3. What should a build record explain? The version, each important change and its reason, current function and unresolved dependencies.\nWatch for: Make each person's output visible. Stop unsafe sections and prevent cosmetic work from replacing assembly or function checks.\nS08 assessment out of 10\nT1 + T2: team evidence /4. I1 + I2: individual work and reasoning /4. C1: individual checking and response /2. For each row, 2 = both conditions, 1 = one, 0 = neither in assessable work; NE = evidence pending.", "route": [{"time": "0–5", "work": "State today's assembly target and outstanding dependencies."}, {"time": "5–15", "work": "Check parts/materials and allocate named outputs."}, {"time": "15–40", "work": "Build or assemble in confirmed safe work areas; follow school-approved access and supervision only."}, {"time": "40–55", "work": "Check fit, movement, sequence or another relevant interface."}, {"time": "55–70", "work": "Correct one observed mismatch and continue assembly."}, {"time": "70–80", "work": "Try the core action safely and identify working, failing and untested sections."}, {"time": "80–90", "work": "Store Prototype V1, update the build record and identify readiness for the internal trial."}], "rubric": [{"id": "T1 Team", "a": "Assembly follows the approved functional design and resource constraints.", "b": "Relevant interfaces are checked rather than assumed to fit."}, {"id": "T2 Team", "a": "The prototype status distinguishes working, failing and untested sections.", "b": "The build record identifies the version and unresolved dependencies."}, {"id": "I1 Individual", "a": "The learner's own build or assembly contribution is verifiable.", "b": "The learner explains its purpose in the core function."}, {"id": "I2 Individual", "a": "The learner describes a specific observed mismatch or build decision.", "b": "The explanation uses measurements, a test observation or a design constraint."}, {"id": "C1 Individual", "a": "The learner performs an identifiable interface or function check.", "b": "The learner records the result and a sensible correction or next check."}]}, {"n": 9, "title": "Run an Internal Test and Peer Handover", "goal": "Test Prototype V1 using repeatable instructions, diagnose one issue and establish safe user-test readiness.", "exit": "Use S09. Internal test and retest, diagnosed issue, peer-handover response and user-test readiness status.", "individual": "One own testing action and an explanation of observation, possible cause and next check.", "example": "Model one chain: observation “the tab catches”; possible cause “the gap may be too small”; one change “adjust the gap”; retest “repeat the same movement”. A check can support a possible cause; one result may not prove it.", "questions": [{"q": "Why change one thing before retesting?", "a": "It helps relate the change to the result while keeping other test conditions comparable."}, {"q": "How is a possible cause different from a finding?", "a": "A finding records what happened; the possible cause is an explanation that still needs a check."}, {"q": "What permits a target-user trial to proceed?", "a": "A safe prototype/task, clear instructions, confirmed users and permissions, and a supervised test arrangement."}], "notes": "Students can: Test Prototype V1 using repeatable instructions, diagnose one issue and establish safe user-test readiness.\nCore exit evidence: Use S09. Internal test and retest, diagnosed issue, peer-handover response and user-test readiness status.\nEach learner: One own testing action and an explanation of observation, possible cause and next check.\nPrepare: S09; the assembled prototype or safe testable section; criteria, written task and results records; the paired-team order and one supervised trial area. Complete safety clearance before handover.\nTeacher control: No fabrication during peer trials. Clear the safe task and visible test area; never rush unsafe prototypes into a user trial.\nSession 9 Teaching notes and assessment\nSafe parallel work or optional stretch: Prepare the next test, verify a recorded measure or examine a contradictory result. A team on Hold tests a safe section only.\nIf the prototype is unsafe or unfinished: restrict the trial to a cleared safe section. Record what is untested. Do not send it to target users merely to reach the next session.\nPeer-review arrangement: Paired teams follow the other team's written task, in the supervised area.\nReviewer questions\n1. Can you carry out the task from the written instructions? Mark the exact missing/ambiguous step.\n2. What result did you obtain against each criterion, and what help was given?\n3. Which observed failure matters most to the core function, and what single-variable test could investigate it?\nReceiving team: Record which finding you acted on, the one change, and the comparable retest result; justify any rejected suggestion.\nTeaching example: Model one chain: observation “the tab catches”; possible cause “the gap may be too small”; one change “adjust the gap”; retest “repeat the same movement”. A check can support a possible cause; one result may not prove it.\nFlex within 10–20: allow up to five minutes to clarify the task and move safely. Shorten repeated briefing, not the comparable retest or individual explanation.\nQuick check with suggested answers\n1. Why change one thing before retesting? It helps relate the change to the result while keeping other test conditions comparable.\n2. How is a possible cause different from a finding? A finding records what happened; the possible cause is an explanation that still needs a check.\n3. What permits a target-user trial to proceed? A safe prototype/task, clear instructions, confirmed users and permissions, and a supervised test arrangement.\nWatch for: Do not run fabrication alongside peer trials. Record assistance and failures, and keep unsafe or unfinished sections out of the user-test task.\nS09 assessment out of 10\nT1 + T2: team evidence /4. I1 + I2: individual work and reasoning /4. C1: individual checking and response /2. For each row, 2 = both conditions, 1 = one, 0 = neither in assessable work; NE = evidence pending.", "route": [{"time": "0–10", "work": "Review criteria, safety, roles and the written task."}, {"time": "10–20", "work": "Rehearse instructions and identify what to measure."}, {"time": "20–45", "work": "Exchange with a paired team for a safe internal task trial; record help and actual results."}, {"time": "45–60", "work": "Choose one issue, propose a cause and change one thing."}, {"time": "60–75", "work": "Retest under comparable conditions and respond to the reviewer."}, {"time": "75–80", "work": "Confirm the safe target-user task or restrict unsafe/unfinished sections."}, {"time": "80–90", "work": "Save results, M3 status and next tasks; store the prototype."}], "rubric": [{"id": "T1 Team", "a": "The internal test uses explicit criteria and recorded conditions.", "b": "Results distinguish evidence of function from appearance and untested claims."}, {"id": "T2 Team", "a": "One issue is investigated through an observation, possible cause and controlled change.", "b": "A comparable retest is recorded without hiding an unfavourable result."}, {"id": "I1 Individual", "a": "The learner's own test or recording action is traceable.", "b": "The learner explains one actual finding accurately."}, {"id": "I2 Individual", "a": "The learner distinguishes a possible cause from a confirmed explanation.", "b": "The learner identifies the next evidence needed to check it."}, {"id": "C1 Individual", "a": "The learner examines a specific peer-handover finding.", "b": "The learner documents the response and resulting change, retest or justified non-change."}]}, {"n": 10, "title": "Test with Target Users", "goal": "Collect genuine task-use evidence without leading users or overstating a small test.", "exit": "Use S10. Target-user task records with conditions, assistance and results, plus one justified improvement priority.", "individual": "One own observation/measurement/briefing contribution and an explanation distinguishing behaviour from opinion.", "example": "Neutral briefing: “Please use this to complete the task. Tell us if you need help.” Record what the user did, what they said and any help separately. “I like it” does not establish that it worked.", "questions": [{"q": "Why avoid coaching users to succeed?", "a": "The team needs to observe what users can understand and do; record any assistance that becomes necessary."}, {"q": "Does a positive opinion establish performance?", "a": "No. Compare observed use with the stated criteria and keep opinions separate."}, {"q": "What limits should the record identify?", "a": "The users, prototype version, conditions, help and number or scope of trials actually represented."}], "notes": "Students can: Collect genuine task-use evidence without leading users or overstating a small test.\nCore exit evidence: Use S10. Target-user task records with conditions, assistance and results, plus one justified improvement priority.\nEach learner: One own observation/measurement/briefing contribution and an explanation distinguishing behaviour from opinion.\nPrepare: S10; the cleared prototype, written task and criteria; confirmed target users, participation permissions and a supervised area; measuring/recording tools. Reconfirm appointments and a fallback task.\nTeacher control: Do not run machinery or send teams elsewhere during trials. If users are unavailable, label internal evidence honestly and record the missing requirement/confirmed next access.\nSession 10 Teaching notes and assessment\nSafe parallel work or optional stretch: Between rounds, check the last record, prepare the next task or compare a result with its criterion.\nIf users cancel: record the gap, verify existing records or rehearse the test. Call peer practice an internal trial, not target-user testing. The school confirms the next access opportunity.\nTeaching example: Neutral briefing: “Please use this to complete the task. Tell us if you need help.” Record what the user did, what they said and any help separately. “I like it” does not establish that it worked.\nFlex within 10–20: reserve up to five minutes for clarification and safe movement. Preserve confirmed user arrangements. Remove repeated explanations before reducing genuine task-use evidence.\nQuick check with suggested answers\n1. Why avoid coaching users to succeed? The team needs to observe what users can understand and do; record any assistance that becomes necessary.\n2. Does a positive opinion establish performance? No. Compare observed use with the stated criteria and keep opinions separate.\n3. What limits should the record identify? The users, prototype version, conditions, help and number or scope of trials actually represented.\nWatch for: Keep all teams visible and machinery closed during user trials. Participation does not itself permit filming or public sharing.\nS10 assessment out of 10\nT1 + T2: team evidence /4. I1 + I2: individual work and reasoning /4. C1: individual checking and response /2. For each row, 2 = both conditions, 1 = one, 0 = neither in assessable work; NE = evidence pending.", "route": [{"time": "0–10", "work": "Check prototype, area, permissions, stop conditions and roles."}, {"time": "10–20", "work": "Rehearse a neutral invitation and task briefing."}, {"time": "20–50", "work": "Run agreed target-user rounds in one visible supervised area."}, {"time": "50–65", "work": "Compare actual results with criteria and starting evidence."}, {"time": "65–80", "work": "Choose one important observed issue and a possible cause for improvement."}, {"time": "80–90", "work": "Save records, contributions and evidence limits; reset the area."}], "rubric": [{"id": "T1 Team", "a": "The record identifies actual task conditions, prototype version and assistance.", "b": "It records behaviour or measures linked to the criteria, not only satisfaction."}, {"id": "T2 Team", "a": "Interpretations and user comments are distinguished from observed results.", "b": "The improvement priority is justified by the recorded task evidence."}, {"id": "I1 Individual", "a": "The learner's observation, measure or briefing contribution is traceable.", "b": "The learner explains their method accurately and without leading claims."}, {"id": "I2 Individual", "a": "The learner distinguishes an opinion from evidence of use.", "b": "The learner states a relevant limit of the users, conditions or number of trials."}, {"id": "C1 Individual", "a": "The learner checks one test record or instruction for bias, omission or inconsistency.", "b": "The learner records a correction or a justified limit on the conclusion."}]}, {"n": 11, "title": "Make an Evidence-led Improvement", "goal": "Select one meaningful change, keep a version record and check its intended effect.", "exit": "Use S11. Prototype V2 or a versioned revised section, evidence-linked reason, predicted effect and a comparable retest plan.", "individual": "One owned revision/check contribution and a reason grounded in user evidence.", "example": "Ask: What will we change? Which result supports it? What should improve? What will stay the same in the retest? Photograph or sketch the old and new version before the change disappears.", "questions": [{"q": "Which issue should be addressed first?", "a": "A safety concern or issue blocking the core function, within what can be safely changed and tested."}, {"q": "What should remain comparable in the retest?", "a": "The task, criterion and relevant conditions, with any differences recorded."}, {"q": "Why retain the earlier version or its record?", "a": "It makes the change traceable and supports a before-and-after comparison."}], "notes": "Students can: Select one meaningful change, keep a version record and check its intended effect.\nCore exit evidence: Use S11. Prototype V2 or a versioned revised section, evidence-linked reason, predicted effect and a comparable retest plan.\nEach learner: One owned revision/check contribution and a reason grounded in user evidence.\nPrepare: S11; actual user-test records; the current prototype/design; a confirmed material and safe production allowance; before-and-after labels and a comparable test plan.\nTeacher control: Do not permit a new whole-project rebuild or extra functions. Recheck safety when materials or operation change.\nSession 11 Teaching notes and assessment\nSafe parallel work or optional stretch: Prepare before/after labels, check constant conditions or analyse the original failing trial while another student revises.\nIf the change needs unconfirmed access: make only an approved feasible revision or prepare it without starting the restricted operation. Results on the shared S11–S12 record are completed after S12 testing, not invented today.\nTeaching example: Ask: What will we change? Which result supports it? What should improve? What will stay the same in the retest? Photograph or sketch the old and new version before the change disappears.\nFlex within 10–20: use up to five minutes to clarify the single change and the prediction. Do not take time from safety checks or move school production arrangements.\nQuick check with suggested answers\n1. Which issue should be addressed first? A safety concern or issue blocking the core function, within what can be safely changed and tested.\n2. What should remain comparable in the retest? The task, criterion and relevant conditions, with any differences recorded.\n3. Why retain the earlier version or its record? It makes the change traceable and supports a before-and-after comparison.\nWatch for: Approve one evidence-led revision. Avoid a whole-project rebuild, additional functions or unconfirmed fabrication dependencies.\nS11 assessment out of 10\nT1 + T2: team evidence /4. I1 + I2: individual work and reasoning /4. C1: individual checking and response /2. For each row, 2 = both conditions, 1 = one, 0 = neither in assessable work; NE = evidence pending.", "route": [{"time": "0–10", "work": "Rank observed issues by safety, core function and user understanding."}, {"time": "10–20", "work": "Select one change, predict its effect and state what stays constant."}, {"time": "20–45", "work": "Make the approved revision with available approved materials and school-confirmed access only."}, {"time": "45–60", "work": "Check the revised section and correct unsafe or obvious assembly problems."}, {"time": "60–75", "work": "Prepare a comparable retest and rehearse its measurement."}, {"time": "75–80", "work": "State the difference between Prototype V1 and Prototype V2 and the prediction."}, {"time": "80–90", "work": "Save the change record, individual evidence and next test task; store work."}], "rubric": [{"id": "T1 Team", "a": "The selected change addresses an important issue in actual test evidence.", "b": "The change stays focused on the core function and feasible resources."}, {"id": "T2 Team", "a": "The revised version is distinguishable from the prior version.", "b": "The retest plan identifies comparable conditions and a relevant measure."}, {"id": "I1 Individual", "a": "The learner's revision or check contribution is verifiable.", "b": "The learner explains its intended effect on the function."}, {"id": "I2 Individual", "a": "The learner links the proposed effect to a specific prior observation.", "b": "The learner identifies a factor that could otherwise confuse the comparison."}, {"id": "C1 Individual", "a": "The learner checks the revised section or measurement procedure.", "b": "The learner records a finding and the resulting correction or readiness decision."}]}, {"n": 12, "title": "Retest and Draw a Limited Conclusion", "goal": "Compare Prototype V1/Prototype V2 evidence fairly and state what improved, worsened or remains uncertain.", "exit": "Use S12. Comparable before/after evidence, justified conclusion, limitations and a bounded remaining evidence question.", "individual": "Explain one comparison and a limitation using their own test/analysis contribution.", "example": "Illustrative conclusion: “In this classroom trial, completion time fell from 20 to 14 seconds.” Do not write “works better for everyone”. If users or conditions changed, state that limit before claiming the revision caused the result.", "questions": [{"q": "Can a different task prove that the revision caused an improvement?", "a": "Not by itself. Identify changed conditions and make the comparison as similar as possible."}, {"q": "What if the result is worse or inconclusive?", "a": "Report the actual comparison, its limits and a useful next test."}, {"q": "What is a defensible conclusion?", "a": "A statement supported by the recorded evidence, limited to the users, task and conditions tested."}], "notes": "Students can: Compare Prototype V1/Prototype V2 evidence fairly and state what improved, worsened or remains uncertain.\nCore exit evidence: Use S12. Comparable before/after evidence, justified conclusion, limitations and a bounded remaining evidence question.\nEach learner: Explain one comparison and a limitation using their own test/analysis contribution.\nPrepare: S12; the earlier results and versioned revision; a comparable task and measuring method; confirmed users if involved; one safe test area and a before-and-after results display.\nTeacher control: Check that claims match actual conditions and that success is not inferred from a different test. Do not penalise an honest failed test merely for failing.\nSession 12 Teaching notes and assessment\nSafe parallel work or optional stretch: Verify calculations/units, examine a contradictory trial or prepare an evidence label; no new feature development.\nIf no fair retest is possible: compare only what the records support, name the changed conditions and keep the required comparison pending. Continue S11’s record; negative results still count as evidence.\nTeaching example: Illustrative conclusion: “In this classroom trial, completion time fell from 20 to 14 seconds.” Do not write “works better for everyone”. If users or conditions changed, state that limit before claiming the revision caused the result.\nFlex within 10–20: allow up to five minutes to clarify units, versions and recording roles. Shorten repeated recap, not testing or the explanation of limits.\nQuick check with suggested answers\n1. Can a different task prove that the revision caused an improvement? Not by itself. Identify changed conditions and make the comparison as similar as possible.\n2. What if the result is worse or inconclusive? Report the actual comparison, its limits and a useful next test.\n3. What is a defensible conclusion? A statement supported by the recorded evidence, limited to the users, task and conditions tested.\nWatch for: Separate target-user retests from internal trials. Assess the quality of investigation and reasoning even when the prototype fails a performance test.\nS12 assessment out of 10\nT1 + T2: team evidence /4. I1 + I2: individual work and reasoning /4. C1: individual checking and response /2. For each row, 2 = both conditions, 1 = one, 0 = neither in assessable work; NE = evidence pending.", "route": [{"time": "0–10", "work": "Check the comparison task, conditions, version and safe readiness."}, {"time": "10–20", "work": "Prepare the results record and confirm who will do each test action."}, {"time": "20–45", "work": "Run the planned retest; use genuine target-user access if confirmed and label internal trials accurately."}, {"time": "45–60", "work": "Compare earlier and current results; flag changed users or conditions."}, {"time": "60–75", "work": "Explain improved, worse, unchanged or inconclusive with evidence and limits."}, {"time": "75–80", "work": "Select the one remaining evidence gap that matters most."}, {"time": "80–90", "work": "Save the comparison, M4 status and individual explanations; store materials."}], "rubric": [{"id": "T1 Team", "a": "The comparison records actual before/after results with version and conditions.", "b": "Differences in users, help or test conditions are identified."}, {"id": "T2 Team", "a": "The conclusion follows from the available comparison, including failure or uncertainty.", "b": "Limitations and a useful next test are specific to the evidence."}, {"id": "I1 Individual", "a": "The learner's retest or analysis work is identifiable.", "b": "The learner accurately explains one numerical or observed comparison."}, {"id": "I2 Individual", "a": "The learner distinguishes a supported conclusion from an overclaim.", "b": "The learner explains a relevant limitation and its effect."}, {"id": "C1 Individual", "a": "The learner checks a comparison, calculation or conclusion against the original records.", "b": "The learner documents a correction or justified confirmation."}]}, {"n": 13, "title": "Challenge the Evidence and Close One Gap", "goal": "Use a structured cross-team audit and a bounded check to strengthen the final explanation.", "exit": "Use S13. Reviewed claim–evidence–limitation set, reasoned peer response and one corrected claim or targeted evidence check.", "individual": "One evidence audit or gap-closing contribution plus an explanation of why it matters.", "example": "Overclaim: “Our design saves everyone time.” Supported version: “In three recorded classroom trials, users completed the task faster with V2; other users have not been tested.” These are example words, not a claim students may copy without matching data.", "questions": [{"q": "What makes a claim traceable?", "a": "An exact record reference showing the result and the conditions under which it was obtained."}, {"q": "What does a photograph of a finished object establish?", "a": "Appearance or assembly at that time; it does not by itself demonstrate operation or effectiveness."}, {"q": "How can an unsupported claim be improved?", "a": "Narrow the wording to what the evidence supports, or conduct one feasible check and record the result."}], "notes": "Students can: Use a structured cross-team audit and a bounded check to strengthen the final explanation.\nCore exit evidence: Use S13. Reviewed claim–evidence–limitation set, reasoned peer response and one corrected claim or targeted evidence check.\nEach learner: One evidence audit or gap-closing contribution plus an explanation of why it matters.\nPrepare: S13; the evolving report, research and test records; the claim–evidence–limitation sequence; the peer audit order and materials for one approved, bounded check.\nTeacher control: Use this lesson for a bounded evidence check. Only safe feasible checks; no new function or unconfirmed production commitment.\nSession 13 Teaching notes and assessment\nSafe parallel work or optional stretch: Trace references, compare captions with records or challenge another unsupported claim. Ready teams test reliability or clarify a limitation.\nIf no safe extra test is feasible: narrow the unsupported claim and record the gap. Do not launch a new build. Carry this checked claim record into S14.\nPeer-review arrangement: Paired-team evidence presentation and audit, with one three-team cycle if needed.\nReviewer questions\n1. Choose one claimed benefit. Which exact test record supports it, and under which conditions?\n2. Does a photograph show operation or merely appearance? Identify what extra evidence would be needed.\n3. Which conclusion overreaches or omits a contradictory result? Propose a narrower claim or one feasible check.\nReceiving team: Log the comment, accept/reject/revise with evidence, and show the corrected claim, completed check or honest unresolved limit.\nTeaching example: Overclaim: “Our design saves everyone time.” Supported version: “In three recorded classroom trials, users completed the task faster with V2; other users have not been tested.” These are example words, not a claim students may copy without matching data.\nFlex within 10–25: use up to five minutes for an example and clarification. Keep the claim presentation brief and spend time checking the actual record, not slide decoration.\nQuick check with suggested answers\n1. What makes a claim traceable? An exact record reference showing the result and the conditions under which it was obtained.\n2. What does a photograph of a finished object establish? Appearance or assembly at that time; it does not by itself demonstrate operation or effectiveness.\n3. How can an unsupported claim be improved? Narrow the wording to what the evidence supports, or conduct one feasible check and record the result.\nWatch for: Keep the lesson focused on evidence gaps and justified wording. Do not use it to add functions or start an unplanned rebuild.\nS13 assessment out of 10\nT1 + T2: team evidence /4. I1 + I2: individual work and reasoning /4. C1: individual checking and response /2. For each row, 2 = both conditions, 1 = one, 0 = neither in assessable work; NE = evidence pending.", "route": [{"time": "0–10", "work": "Audit report sections, test records and one remaining evidence gap."}, {"time": "10–25", "work": "Prepare a brief claim–evidence–limitation presentation with exact record references."}, {"time": "25–45", "work": "Paired teams challenge claims, trace evidence and check whether conclusions exceed results."}, {"time": "45–65", "work": "Close one feasible evidence gap through a safe targeted check or correct the unsupported claim."}, {"time": "65–80", "work": "Record responses and organise the final evidence sequence."}, {"time": "80–90", "work": "Save the review and individual contributions; set final communication tasks and store work."}], "rubric": [{"id": "T1 Team", "a": "Key claims are traceable to actual research or test records.", "b": "The evidence sequence includes relevant limitations or contradictory results."}, {"id": "T2 Team", "a": "The team addresses a meaningful evidence gap or corrects an unsupported claim.", "b": "The resulting explanation stays within the scope of what was actually tested."}, {"id": "I1 Individual", "a": "The learner's own audit or targeted-check contribution is identifiable.", "b": "The learner explains the importance of that evidence to a final claim."}, {"id": "I2 Individual", "a": "The learner identifies a specific mismatch between claim and evidence or justifies a sound match.", "b": "The learner proposes a feasible correction or appropriately limited wording."}, {"id": "C1 Individual", "a": "The learner engages with a precise cross-team evidence challenge.", "b": "The learner records a reasoned response and its outcome."}]}, {"n": 14, "title": "Finish the Report Video and Individual Evidence", "goal": "Communicate one coherent project journey using verified evidence and individual explanations.", "exit": "Use S14. One report, a roughly three-minute process video, each learner's portfolio/evidence explanation and access/playback checks.", "individual": "Own work evidence and one decision/learning explanation plus a named communication task.", "example": "Use six story parts: problem and user evidence; alternatives and choice; build and core function; tests and change; conclusion and limits; sources and contributions. Match each part to an existing record or image. A clear, supported caption matters more than an effect.", "questions": [{"q": "What should the journey video explain?", "a": "The problem, evidence, alternatives, build, tests and change, then the conclusion and limits."}, {"q": "What makes individual evidence distinct?", "a": "It identifies that learner's actual work, reasoning and learning with a traceable example."}, {"q": "What can replace a missing process clip?", "a": "An authentic photograph or record with narration or captions; never stage a test result that did not occur."}], "notes": "Students can: Communicate one coherent project journey using verified evidence and individual explanations.\nCore exit evidence: Use S14. One report, a roughly three-minute process video, each learner's portfolio/evidence explanation and access/playback checks.\nEach learner: Own work evidence and one decision/learning explanation plus a named communication task.\nPrepare: S14; the existing report records, permitted images/clips and source list; each learner's evidence; school-approved editing tools, playback device and internal submission channel. Confirm presentation slots.\nTeacher control: Keep content accurate and within the actual screening slots. Reports are assembled from prior work, not started from blank pages; complex editing is unnecessary.\nSession 14 Teaching notes and assessment\nSafe parallel work or optional stretch: If one person edits, others verify claims, captions, sources or own evidence. With no device, prepare the same storyboard on paper and record the video export as pending.\nIf a device or export fails: finish the storyboard, captions and authentic still-image backup on paper or an available approved device. Keep video export pending. Continue S13’s claims and the handover record used again in S15.\nTeaching example: Use six story parts: problem and user evidence; alternatives and choice; build and core function; tests and change; conclusion and limits; sources and contributions. Match each part to an existing record or image. A clear, supported caption matters more than an effect.\nFlex within 10–25: allow up to five minutes for file movement and clarification. Reduce decorative editing or repeated prose. Protect each learner’s 25–35 evidence explanation and playback/privacy checks.\nQuick check with suggested answers\n1. What should the journey video explain? The problem, evidence, alternatives, build, tests and change, then the conclusion and limits.\n2. What makes individual evidence distinct? It identifies that learner's actual work, reasoning and learning with a traceable example.\n3. What can replace a missing process clip? An authentic photograph or record with narration or captions; never stage a test result that did not occur.\nWatch for: Give non-editors named evidence, source, caption or access checks. Keep simulations and actual function clearly labelled, and do not publish publicly without permission.\nS14 assessment out of 10\nT1 + T2: team evidence /4. I1 + I2: individual work and reasoning /4. C1: individual checking and response /2. For each row, 2 = both conditions, 1 = one, 0 = neither in assessable work; NE = evidence pending.", "route": [{"time": "0–10", "work": "Check the report, source evidence, video assets and individual records."}, {"time": "10–25", "work": "Arrange six story parts: problem/user evidence, alternatives/choice, build/function, tests/change, conclusion/limits, sources/contributions."}, {"time": "25–35", "work": "Each learner selects own evidence and explains a significant decision and learning."}, {"time": "35–60", "work": "Complete named tasks for the single report, captions/script, simple video and source/privacy checks."}, {"time": "60–75", "work": "Exchange work to check evidence accuracy, permitted sharing and actual playback/access."}, {"time": "75–80", "work": "Fix the most important content or access issue."}, {"time": "80–90", "work": "Save review-ready final files and a real still-image backup; reset the workspace."}], "rubric": [{"id": "T1 Team", "a": "The report connects the one project's problem, choices, tests and conclusion to actual evidence.", "b": "It includes relevant limitations and credits borrowed sources."}, {"id": "T2 Team", "a": "The video sequence explains development and changes rather than only displaying the product.", "b": "Images/captions distinguish actual function, simulations and untested claims."}, {"id": "I1 Individual", "a": "The learner selects verifiable evidence of their own work.", "b": "The learner explains their specific contribution beyond a general role label."}, {"id": "I2 Individual", "a": "The learner explains a decision influenced by evidence.", "b": "The learner identifies a concrete learning point grounded in that decision."}, {"id": "C1 Individual", "a": "The learner performs an identifiable content, source/privacy, access or playback check.", "b": "The learner records its result and correction or justified confirmation."}]}, {"n": 15, "title": "Share Results Review and Reflect", "goal": "Explain results, give evidence-based feedback and reflect on individual learning.", "exit": "Use S15. Final accessible submissions, evidence-linked presentation, peer feedback/response and individual reflection.", "individual": "An evidence note or question for each presentation, detailed feedback to one team and a specific own-learning reflection.", "example": "Model useful feedback: “I saw the recorded failed trial. It shows the original tab caught. Next, test the revised tab several times under the same task.” For reflection, ask for one own action, the evidence and what the learner would do differently.", "questions": [{"q": "What makes feedback useful?", "a": "A specific observed evidence item, an explanation and a relevant next test or improvement."}, {"q": "How should a learner explain their progress?", "a": "Use their own work and a decision that evidence changed or confirmed, then name a better future method."}, {"q": "What if a video fails to play?", "a": "Use the authentic still-image backup within the same slot and separately resolve the file-access issue."}], "notes": "Students can: Explain results, give evidence-based feedback and reflect on individual learning.\nCore exit evidence: Use S15. Final accessible submissions, evidence-linked presentation, peer feedback/response and individual reflection.\nEach learner: An evidence note or question for each presentation, detailed feedback to one team and a specific own-learning reflection.\nPrepare: S15; final files and a still-image backup; the screening order and timer; evidence-note and feedback records; a verified internal submission location. Test playback and access before class.\nTeacher control: Keep time; a failed video uses the still-image backup within the same slot. Separate shared presentation evidence from individual explanations and feedback.\nSession 15 Teaching notes and assessment\nSafe parallel work or optional stretch: During changeovers, finish the evidence note or prepare a testable question. Early submitters check their references and final receipt.\nIf a video fails: show the authentic still-image backup within the same presentation slot. Record access repair separately. Finish the existing S14–S15 handover record rather than duplicating it.\nPeer-review arrangement: Class showcase; every learner gives one detailed comment to another team and teams respond to one chosen comment.\nReviewer questions\n1. Name an exact observed result or evidence item from the presentation.\n2. What does it support, and what uncertainty remains?\n3. Propose one next test with a task and observable result, linked to that uncertainty.\nReceiving team: State which suggestion is useful, why, and the evidence needed before deciding; a post-course rebuild is not required.\nTeaching example: Model useful feedback: “I saw the recorded failed trial. It shows the original tab caught. Next, test the revised tab several times under the same task.” For reflection, ask for one own action, the evidence and what the learner would do differently.\nThe 15–55 showcase already includes at least ten minutes of changeover/delay capacity for up to six five-minute presentations. Do not add a second buffer or reduce individual reflection at 65–80.\nQuick check with suggested answers\n1. What makes feedback useful? A specific observed evidence item, an explanation and a relevant next test or improvement.\n2. How should a learner explain their progress? Use their own work and a decision that evidence changed or confirmed, then name a better future method.\n3. What if a video fails to play? Use the authentic still-image backup within the same slot and separately resolve the file-access issue.\nWatch for: Keep each presentation within its slot. Use shared project evidence for team checks and each learner's own explanation and feedback for individual checks.\nS15 assessment out of 10\nT1 + T2: team evidence /4. I1 + I2: individual work and reasoning /4. C1: individual checking and response /2. For each row, 2 = both conditions, 1 = one, 0 = neither in assessable work; NE = evidence pending.", "route": [{"time": "0–10", "work": "Check receipt and access for the report, video and every individual's evidence."}, {"time": "10–15", "work": "Review viewing tasks and fixed presentation slots."}, {"time": "15–55", "work": "Screen in order; for up to six teams, allow up to five minutes each including the approximately three-minute video, short evidence question and changeover; retain buffer."}, {"time": "55–65", "work": "Give detailed evidence-linked feedback; receiving teams select a suggestion and the next evidence needed."}, {"time": "65–80", "work": "Reflect individually on own work and one decision changed by evidence."}, {"time": "80–90", "work": "Add feedback/reflection, confirm final receipt and store materials."}], "rubric": [{"id": "T1 Team", "a": "The presentation and final report make consistent claims supported by evidence.", "b": "The team states limitations and answers an evidence question without overclaiming."}, {"id": "T2 Team", "a": "The project explanation connects problem, alternatives, building, tests and revision.", "b": "The submitted evidence permits the stated learning and results to be checked."}, {"id": "I1 Individual", "a": "The learner identifies specific own work and its contribution.", "b": "The learner supports the explanation with a verifiable record or example."}, {"id": "I2 Individual", "a": "The learner explains how evidence changed or confirmed a decision.", "b": "The learner proposes a concrete improvement to their own future method."}, {"id": "C1 Individual", "a": "The learner gives feedback tied to an exact observed evidence item.", "b": "The learner provides a reasoned next test or response rather than only praise or preference."}]}], "intro": "M3 Innovation Challenge\nTeacher Guide Semester 2\nPlan for 15 taught sessions of 90 minutes (22.5 hours). Students develop one school-based project through five milestones. This guide gives the learning goals, core evidence, suggested lesson routes and assessment. Adapt delivery to the actual class; do not change safety rules or treat missing evidence as completed work.\nStart here\nBefore each lesson, read that session’s goal, exit evidence and preparation. Show students only the current step and its answer space. Model one short example, ask a learner to explain the task back, then let the class begin.\nWho does what\nCurriculum team: maintain clear goals, usable core materials, consistent assessment and examples. Revise shared wording or structure when feedback shows a recurring difficulty.\nClass teacher: check the actual learners and resources, prepare the lesson, choose appropriate examples and language support, adjust grouping and pace, and check learning. Class-specific adaptation remains part of teaching; unclear core materials should still be reported and improved.\nSchool and Innovation Center: control facility access, machine queues, production times, authorised operators and local safety rules. This guide does not allocate or change those arrangements. Use only what the school confirms.\nUse time without losing the learning\nCore work is the session’s exit evidence and each learner’s own contribution. Optional depth comes after it: an extra relevant trial, stronger explanation or clearer instruction. Decoration and repeated copying are not required.\nTreat the minute plan as a teaching route, not a guaranteed completion time or machine booking. Allow clarification and movement inside the lesson. Reduce repeated explanation or optional depth first; keep safety checks, individual thinking, genuine evidence and safe close. If core work cannot finish, record what is missing and resume it.\nAt the end, save three things on the existing record: what is finished, the next named task and what is blocking it. Do not create another report. Use NE for evidence that cannot yet be judged, with a next opportunity and review date.\nReading this guide\nRead the course preparation and assessment once. Then use the two-page spread for today’s session: lesson route first, teaching notes and assessment second. The printing map explains which learner pages to copy and which shared records to continue.\nCourse at a glance\nStudents investigate one real problem in the school, define a reachable user group and pilot setting, compare solutions, and develop successive versions of the same prototype. They gather evidence from use, make a justified improvement and communicate what the results show. Maintain one evolving team report, one journey video and each student's own evidence record throughout the project.\nStudents have previous experience with 3D printing and laser cutting. Check what they can do safely before allocating tasks. Review only the skills needed for the selected route; do not assume prior Scratch or micro:bit experience. Teams choose tools to suit a clear function and can combine a small fabricated component with approved materials.\nThe project must include a contribution students design or develop themselves. Existing examples can inform their work when sources and adaptations are identified. The prototype needs a demonstrable core function and a realistic test. Unsuccessful or inconclusive results must remain in the record and can still support a sound investigation.\nUse the milestone gates to decide the next task. Record Ready, Revise or Hold with one specific action. A gate decision concerns the work described; equipment still requires its own job-specific permission.\nDocumented gaps in required facility or target-user evidence remain pending requirements. Teams may continue safe work and report limited conclusions, but record those requirements as fulfilled only when the required evidence is available.\nDesign Thinking connects understanding users, defining the problem, exploring ideas, prototyping and testing. Teams return to an earlier step when evidence challenges a decision. Students recall tool limits, apply design skills, analyse findings, evaluate alternatives and create revisions throughout the course.\nBefore teaching begins\nClass and language: check the roster, actual lesson length and students’ reading needs. For the planned 20–30 learners, organise 4–6 stable teams of 4–5. For 25 learners, use five teams of five. Assign work tables, a storage place and one shared record per team. Adjust to the actual roster.\nPeople and materials: confirm the teacher’s training and authorisation, available tools and materials, budget or equal allocation, room layout, storage and school safety rules. Plan for one teacher. Include another operator only when the school has confirmed one.\nConfirm the school-provided Innovation Center arrangements and which tools or processes are genuinely available. Note any access or supervision that is still unconfirmed. Do not calculate or allocate the school’s production schedule, job order or machine queues. Base student plans on confirmed access; keep affected work pending when access is unknown.\nUsers and space: confirm one safe observation or testing area and reachable users. Keep whole-class visits together or bring genuine user evidence into the classroom. Never send unsupervised teams around school. Prepare an honest alternative if access changes.\nDevices and privacy: confirm permitted recording devices, participation/photography permissions, internal submission access and video playback. Use roles or user codes. Do not collect identifying images or audio without permission.\nAssessment: check the school’s use of the 10-point lesson record, including absence and missing evidence. The course does not set official term-grade weighting. Prepare a short example and the five observable checks for today; do not teach every future code at once.\nClassroom routines for one teacher\nVisible plan: At the start, display today's workbook ID, minimum exit evidence and deadlines. Each team updates one board or paper record showing its current task, next task, help request and evidence ready for checking.\nNamed roles: Allocate coordinating, design/build, testing and evidence responsibilities. In teams of five, separate materials and timekeeping from coordination. Rotate responsibilities between sessions. Every student produces a traceable contribution; a role title is not evidence.\nHelp routine: read the current step, ask a teammate and try one safe check. Then ask one precise question and continue the stated safe task. A safety concern bypasses this routine: stop the affected activity and tell the teacher immediately.\nTeacher rounds: show the order in which tables will be checked. Give one next action per routine visit, then move on. Sample explanations while students work and complete individual marks from their records. Do not expect every learner to give a separate final presentation. Safety checks are never shortened to fit a review timer.\nWork status: Mark the next step Ready, Revise or Hold on the existing record. State what must change and when it will be checked again. Scope approval does not authorise machine use. Teams on Hold continue the approved low-risk task listed for that session.\nFinish and reset: Rehearse one attention signal in S01. At the stated stop time, follow safe tool shutdown procedures, label and store work, clear tables and submit the exit evidence. Start shutdown earlier when the equipment requires it.\nEarly finishers: Check the required evidence first. Then repeat an uncertain measurement, test a harder relevant condition or improve an instruction and try it with a reader. Do not add features, print extra copies or start another tool without approval.\nSet a concrete research, design, building, testing or reasoning output for every learner. Materials collection and timekeeping support the team but do not replace the individual evidence required for assessment.\nOn returning from absence, the learner reads the latest team record, explains the latest decision and takes a concrete individual task with an equivalent opportunity to demonstrate any missing assessed skill.\nSafe Innovation Center use\nEach team must actually use an available Innovation Center tool or process and explain what it contributed to the prototype. A facility tour or an unproduced design alone does not complete this requirement. Record the tool or process used, the part or function it supported and the resulting evidence.\nOnly a trained, authorised person operates specialist machinery under the centre's rules. When the teacher is the only adult, use only equipment the teacher is authorised to supervise and operate. Student leaders do not replace an adult supervisor.\nRun one controlled machine activity at a time unless the centre's risk assessment explicitly permits another arrangement. Close the ordinary help queue during active supervision. Other students complete named non-hazardous work in a safely supervisable area.\nIf the layout or operation prevents safe supervision of the rest of the class, do not begin that arrangement. Ask the school to confirm an acceptable arrangement. Meanwhile use an approved non-machine task; do not assume another slot is available.\nFollow the school’s current access instructions and file requirements. Students prepare the required files or materials and continue a named safe task while waiting. Do not publish a new production order or alter school queues. Do not leave machinery running while the supervising teacher moves away to teach another group.\nUse a small, safe model or critical section to keep design and test preparation moving when production is delayed. Record what it cannot yet demonstrate. Actual facility use remains a required pending item; only the school can confirm another access opportunity. Do not assume out-of-class fabrication is available.\nBefore S06, check the school’s confirmed access, approved materials and file requirements. Record what is confirmed, what remains unavailable and the safe work students can do meanwhile. Recheck these dependencies after a design or material changes; do not set machine cycles, production durations or replacement slots.\nAssessment in every taught class\nEach learner can earn 10 points: 4 from shared team evidence, 4 from their own work and reasoning, and 2 from their own checking and response. Announce the five checks before work starts. Use the specific quality conditions in that session’s table and the matching workbook section.\nEvery check has two observable quality conditions. Award 2 when both are demonstrated, 1 when exactly one is demonstrated, and 0 when neither is demonstrated in the assessable work. Points depend on evidence quality and reasoning; filling every box alone does not earn them. Add T1 + T2 + I1 + I2 + C1 for the learner’s total out of 10.\nAssess T1 and T2 once from the shared team work and show members that subtotal. Assess I1, I2 and C1 separately for every learner from their own traceable work or recorded explanation. During work, sample explanations at table visits; use the submitted records to complete the individual scores.\nUse NE, meaning not enough evidence yet, where a judgement cannot be made. Record the reason, the affected check and an equivalent evidence opportunity. Absence, inaccessible files, unavailable equipment or a cancelled user visit does not alone establish low achievement. NE stays pending until evidence supports a numeric score; it is not zero.\nAccept agreed learning supports and suitable written, spoken or visual evidence of the same skill. Judge the stated skill, not attendance, speed, speaking confidence, expensive materials or complex editing. An unsuccessful test can earn credit for sound methods and reasoning. Equipment permission and safety remain separate requirements at every score.\nUse one class assessment record: actual class/date | workbook section or combined sections | selected rubric ID | learner | T1 | T2 | I1 | I2 | C1 | total out of 10 or pending | evidence reference | next action/review date. Record the evidence and date when a score is reassessed.\nThe maximum is 150 only when all 15 assessed classes are taught. If fewer classes are taught, the available total is 10 × the number of assessed classes. A school-event cancellation is recorded as not taught, with no student score. Conversion to an official school grade or weighting follows school policy.\nKeeping milestones flexible\nSchedule milestones rather than fixing each topic to a calendar week. At an interruption, save the last completed evidence, next named task and blocker in the workbook Resume line. Reopen that record at the next taught class. Move dates first; when a missed class cannot be replaced, choose an eligible two-class route below or reduce the project scope.\nEach two-class route still contains two 90-minute classes. Use it only when all readiness conditions are met; otherwise retain or reschedule the third class. Preserve safety, genuine facility use, individual evidence and a meaningful test–revision–retest cycle. Reduce repeated briefings, model detail, duplicate writing and decorative editing before cutting essential evidence.\nWorkbook IDs remain content references when two sections share a class. Publish the single rubric selected below before that class. Award one score out of 10 for the actual class, not one score for each worksheet section. The other section’s activity and safety requirements remain part of the work.\nM1 two-class route\nUse when: Users/observation are reachable in the teaching area; the actual facility orientation can be completed safely in the allocated time; research does not need a separate off-site visit.\nRetain: Safety understanding remains a required permission check even though the numeric Class A evidence uses S02. Keep the evidence-backed brief, two criteria and peer response; remove repeated introductions, not research.\nM2 two-class route\nUse when: The scoped brief and criteria are ready; approved low-cost modelling materials are ready; quick models can genuinely check the chosen uncertainty.\nRetain: Keep both individual sketches, two alternatives, trial evidence, peer response and production gate. Shorten modelling detail and presentation polish, not the comparison.\nM3 two-class route\nUse when: designs/files and materials are ready, the school confirms a safe route that supports the necessary work, and no training, supervision or access issue remains unresolved. Do not choose this shorter route on an assumed production time.\nRetain: job-specific permission checks, genuine center-tool/process use, a safe testable core and peer/internal retest. The teaching blocks below do not book or limit machine time. If school-confirmed access does not support the route, retain or reschedule the third taught class.\nM4 two-class route\nUse when: Prototype V1 is safe, user access is confirmed, and the evidence-led change is feasible with available low-risk materials without depending on unconfirmed facility access.\nRetain: genuine user evidence, the reason for the change, a comparable retest and limitations. If the necessary change cannot be completed safely under confirmed school arrangements, retain the third class.\nM5 two-class route\nUse when: Research/testing evidence and draft report sections are already complete; media assets are available; no unresolved core-function test needs a new build. Use a simple evidence-led video.\nRetain: Keep the peer claim check, report, video, own-learning evidence and final showcase. Cut decorative editing and duplicate prose. If new physical evidence is essential, keep S13 as a separate third class.\nUsing the student workbook\nKeep one team record and each learner’s own response. S01–S15 are parts of the same project. Assemble these records as the final report; do not rewrite the same data.\nIntroduce codes when used: E for research, A/B for alternatives, V0/V1/V2 for versions, TR for tests and SC1/SC2 for success criteria. T1/T2/I1/I2/C1 are assessment checks. Explain the action before the code.\nShow a short example and the response space. Ask a learner to restate the output and safe next step if blocked. Accept agreed short notes, sketches or spoken evidence of the same skill; keep each learner’s contribution identifiable.\nPair teams for review; use one three-team cycle if needed. Review the named record, cite evidence and give one specific comment. The receiving team records its response, reason and action. Keep trials in the approved visible area.\nPrinting and continuing the learner records\nUse the revised packs listed below. Print personal pages for each learner, preferably double-sided. Print shared pages once per team. Display directions pages or provide one team reference; do not print extra personal copies. Keep each learner’s work identifiable.\nReuse means continue the original evidence, not copy it again. Add a fresh dated row or a spare copy only when more space is needed. Keep prior results, including failures. The class teacher may use equivalent accessible formats without changing the assessed skill.\nM1 Understand Users and Define the Problem\nMilestone gate: Evidence-backed problem; one user group and pilot context; two observable criteria and a test method.\n", "final": "Final evidence and submission\nSubmit one evolving team report, the final prototype and relevant earlier-version evidence, one journey video, and each learner’s own evidence and reflection. The report uses the workbook records developed during the course. Show the final status honestly, including what does not yet work or has not been tested.\nTeam report structure\nProject and members: Project title, members, responsibilities and important role changes.\nProblem and evidence: The user group, pilot context, actual incidents and research evidence, sources and remaining assumptions.\nSuccess criteria: SC1 and SC2, the tasks and conditions used to check them, and the starting measure or stated comparison limit.\nAlternatives and choice: Designs A and B, the quick-model comparison, borrowed sources and the team’s own development.\nProduction and versions: The approved route, actual Innovation Center tool/process contribution, materials, build record and Prototype V0/Prototype V1/Prototype V2 changes.\nTesting and revision: TR records showing conditions, help, actual results, the reason for one change and a comparable retest.\nConclusion and review: Supported conclusions, limitations, peer comments with the team’s responses and a useful next test.\nVideo and individual evidence\nUse approximately three minutes for the journey video, adjusted to the actual screening schedule. Explain the problem, evidence, alternatives, building, tests and change, then the conclusion and limitations. Authentic photographs, captions and simple clips are sufficient. Credit sources and distinguish simulated features from actual results.\nEach learner identifies their own research, design, build, testing or interpretation work and explains an important decision and learning point using a verifiable record. A general role label or identical reflection copied across the team is insufficient. Keep individual scores and explanations visible separately from the shared report.\nBefore submission, open the report and video through the internal submission channel, verify the teacher can access each learner’s evidence, test playback and retain an authentic still-image backup. Participation or classroom screening does not authorise public posting; follow the permissions already confirmed.\n", "reference": "When a team cannot move forward\nMilestone\nFull-route sections\nEvidence required to move on\nM1 Understand Users and Define the Problem\nS01–S03\nEvidence-backed problem; one user group and pilot context; two observable criteria and a test method.\nM2 Explore Ideas and Plan the Prototype\nS04–S06\nTwo approaches compared; chosen concept justified; scope, safe Innovation Center route, resource limit, test plan and functional fallback agreed.\nM3 Build and Test the Core Function\nS07–S09\nSafe testable prototype or critical section; verified tool/process use; internal test and diagnosed issue; readiness for user testing.\nM4 Test with Users and Improve\nS10–S12\nActual user evidence or an explicitly documented evidence gap; versioned change; comparable retest; defensible conclusion and limitations.\nM5 Verify Evidence and Communicate Learning\nS13–S15\nOne report, process video, individual evidence and reflection; peer feedback with a reasoned response; submission access checked.\nMain criterion\nChecks\nMaximum\nTeam evidence\nT1 + T2\n4\nIndividual work and reasoning\nI1 + I2\n4\nIndividual checking and response\nC1\n2\nClass\n90-minute plan and assessment\nA\nUse S01 + S02. 0–10: One-project brief and named team tasks; 10–30: Whole-class facility orientation and safety/stop check; 30–45: Neutral-question practice and research plan; 45–65: Supervised evidence collection; 65–80: Sort evidence and record the starting situation; 80–90: Individual explanation, storage and exit. Apply S02 (all five checks).\nB\nUse the full 90-minute S03 plan below. Apply S03 (all five checks).\nClass\n90-minute plan and assessment\nA\nUse S04 + S05. 0–5: State the comparison task; 5–15: Individual two-approach sketches; 15–25: Select two distinct team approaches; 25–45: Make quick models of the uncertain part; 45–65: Cross-team trials under the same task; 65–80: Compare and choose with evidence; 80–90: Peer response, individual explanation and storage. Apply S05 (all five checks).\nB\nUse the full 90-minute S06 plan below. Apply S06 (all five checks).\nClass\n90-minute plan and assessment\nA\nUse S07 + S08. 0–10: Confirm outputs and school-provided access arrangements; 10–25: Critical-part trial and job-specific checks; 25–60: Confirmed safe fabrication and assembly with named parallel work; 60–80: Check interfaces and core-function status; 80–90: Shutdown/storage and individual build evidence. Apply S08 (all five checks).\nB\nUse the full 90-minute S09 plan below. Apply S09 (all five checks).\nClass\n90-minute plan and assessment\nA\nUse S10 + S11. 0–10: Safety, permissions and roles; 10–40: Target-user task trials; 40–55: Analyse results and select one change; 55–80: Make the feasible versioned change and prepare retesting; 80–90: Record evidence, individual contribution and store. Apply S10 (all five checks).\nB\nUse the full 90-minute S12 plan below. Apply S12 (all five checks).\nClass\n90-minute plan and assessment\nA\nUse S13 + S14. 0–10: Audit available evidence and claims; 10–25: Cross-team evidence challenge; 25–40: Correct claims and record responses; 40–50: Complete own evidence/learning explanation; 50–75: Assemble report and simple video from existing material; 75–80: Playback and access check; 80–90: Save deliverables and still-image backup. Apply S14 (all five checks).\nB\nUse the full 90-minute S15 plan below. Apply S15 (all five checks).\nPack\nPages to print or reuse\nS01\nPersonal pages 1 and 3; one team page 2.\nS02–S03\nFor each session: personal pages 1 and 3; one team page 2.\nS04–S05\nPersonal pages 1 and 4 in each session. Start team pages 2–3 in S04 and continue that same comparison record in S05.\nS06\nOne team copy of pages 1–2; personal page 3.\nS07–S08\nPersonal page 3 in each session. Start team pages 1–2 in S07; continue the same build record in S08.\nS09–S10\nFor each session: one team copy of pages 1–2; personal page 3.\nS11–S12\nDisplay page 1; personal page 3 in each session. Start team page 2 in S11 and continue it in S12.\nS13\nDisplay page 1; one team page 2; personal page 3.\nS14\nDisplay page 1; personal page 4. Continue S13’s claim record on page 2; start the team handover record on page 3.\nS15\nDisplay page 1; personal pages 3–4. Continue S14’s handover record, reproduced here on page 2.\n", "troubleshooting": "What you see\nCheck first\nNext safe task\nA product idea with weak evidence\nWhich actual user incident supports the need?\nReturn to an approved observation or source record; revise the claim before expanding the design.\nAn oversized design\nWhich function is essential, and which resources are confirmed?\nRemove extras and plan a small necessary fabricated component or a feasible functional route.\nA fabrication delay\nIs the file ready, and what access and supervision has the school actually confirmed?\nContinue approved fit, assembly, testing or evidence work. Keep actual facility use pending; ask the school to confirm access.\nA failed or unsafe test\nWhat happened, under which conditions, and is it safe to continue?\nStop unsafe use. Examine one possible cause, change one thing and retest safely.\nUsers cancel\nWhich user access is actually available?\nRecord the missing target-user evidence and a confirmed next opportunity; label any internal trial accurately.\nOne person doing the main work\nCan each learner show a specific contribution?\nReassign concrete design, research, build or testing outputs and rotate roles; check individual evidence.\nGeneric peer comments\nWhich step, criterion or evidence record does the comment address?\nRequire one precise observation, a reason and a proposed check; record the receiving team’s response.\nMissing evidence near submission\nDoes the evidence exist, and can its source be traced?\nRetrieve actual records or conduct one feasible check. Narrow unsupported claims rather than inventing results.\n", "source": "https://docs.google.com/document/d/1ICWzrKMvbLfOa-6gVZMjfMAuu0IhIbET/edit"};
